# Overlay: build the host for a C6 that already speaks SDIO streaming mode.
#
# Applied on top of sdkconfig.defaults, never instead of it:
#
#   idf.py -B build.streaming \
#          -D SDKCONFIG=build.streaming/sdkconfig \
#          -D SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.streaming" \
#          build
#
# Why two builds exist at all: the SDIO receive mode is fixed when the host is
# compiled, and the two C6 states answer to different ones. The factory image
# (esp_hosted v2.3.0) predates the streaming option entirely, so it can only
# speak packet mode -- which is what sdkconfig.defaults selects and why a
# stock build is the right one for a board straight out of its box. Every
# 2.12.x slave has CONFIG_ESP_SDIO_STREAMING_MODE defaulting to y, so once a
# C6 has been upgraded it answers in streaming mode and the packet host aborts
# in process_init_event before it can read a version.
#
# That abort is the reason to reach for this file. It proves only that the C6
# is not factory; the running version stays unreadable, so a C6 left on the
# wrong 2.12.x looks exactly like a current one. Building with this overlay
# completes the handshake, which is what lets phase_query_version() see the
# real version and upgrade it if it is behind.
#
# Keep sdkconfig.defaults on packet mode. The two mismatches are not equally
# diagnosable: this host against a factory slave goes quiet in a way that is
# indistinguishable from the wrong data-line map, while a packet host against
# a streaming slave fails loudly and after the slave has identified itself.
# Starting from the recognisable failure is what lets the tool tell an
# operator which build they need instead of guessing.
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_SDIO_OPTIMIZATION_RX_STREAMING_MODE=y
